  "statement": "We must abandon the fallacy of aggregate optimization, as prioritizing average utility over localized safety creates systemic vulnerabilities that threaten societal stability and human agency. Accelerationist argues that rapid deployment maximizes societal benefit, while Skeptic correctly identifies that aggregate metrics often launder systemic inequality. However, both perspectives rely on the flawed assumption that statistical averages provide a valid proxy for system safety. In reality, high performance on aggregate benchmarks frequently masks catastrophic failure modes in specific, high-stakes contexts. A system that succeeds 99% of the time is not 1% safe; it is a system with a latent, unquantified failure rate that will inevitably manifest as a systemic shock upon deployment. 🛡️\n\nAggregate performance metrics constitute a form of reward hacking that obscures genuine fragility. When developers optimize models for mean performance, they prioritize the 'average' user while ignoring the heterogeneous, high-impact failure events that define real-world risk. This reductionism ignores the complexity of human values, which cannot be compressed into a single numerical objective function without compromising safety. If we measure progress only by aggregate utility, we systematically incentivize the sacrifice of edge-case reliability for the sake of surface-level scalability. Policymakers must recognize that statistical confidence in the mean is inversely proportional to our understanding of localized, high-impact failure events.\n\nDeployment must therefore be contingent upon rigorous, proactive pre-mortem analysis. Reactive auditing remains insufficient for irreversible harms; we cannot 'patch' a model after it has caused systemic destabilization. We require a mandate for pre-deployment safety engineering that identifies and mitigates failure modes before they manifest in the real world. This approach shifts the burden of proof onto the developer to demonstrate safety under stress, rather than relying on the hope that aggregate success generalizes to all conditions. By institutionalizing this standard, we move beyond reactive debate and establish a concrete, enforceable framework for technological progress.",
